// Cryptography, bound to Monocypher.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because Monocypher depends on nothing -- it includes `<stddef.h>`
// and `<stdint.h>` and that is the whole list.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       monocypher { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/monocypher", version = "0.2.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.2.1` on this repository. `build-lib` and `--lib` still work and are what the tests here
// use, but they are no longer the only way in.

// `name` is the package; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.monocypher`, and the two are
// deliberately different. The module carries the `sh.sysl.` prefix so that no package claims a
// top-level word another one might want; the package name is the short one, because it names this
// repository rather than a namespace.

package {
  name    = "monocypher"
  version = "0.2.1"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block, and that is the point of this package.**
//
// Monocypher includes `<stddef.h>` and `<stdint.h>` and nothing else -- verified against the
// vendored 4.0.3 source, not assumed from its README. It opens no file, reads no clock, calls no
// allocator and asks the operating system for nothing, so there is no capability to name.
//
// linenoise, the other vendored-C package, needs `posix = true` because it puts a terminal into raw
// mode. The contrast is the useful part: a capability clause is a claim about what a library *does*,
// and a library that does nothing but arithmetic on bytes it was handed makes no claim at all. This
// is what lets the package build for a freestanding target.
//
// The one thing it cannot supply is randomness -- see the README. That is not a capability sysl
// could grant it either; it is an input the caller has to bring.
